WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:01.620
- Thanks to Curiosity Stream for keeping

00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:02.760
Legal Eagle in the air.

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:05.620
Get 26% off CuriosityStream and Nebula

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:07.717
using the link in the description.

00:00:07.717 --> 00:00:10.685
(whooshing) Everyone knows
police can't lie to you.

00:00:10.685 --> 00:00:12.854
(buzzer buzzing) False.

00:00:12.854 --> 00:00:15.850
(dramatic music)

00:00:15.850 --> 00:00:18.060
If you ask a lawyer a legal question,

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:20.400
the answer is usually, "It depends,"

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:22.850
because it usually depends.

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:25.090
The law is a vast and complex thing.

00:00:25.090 --> 00:00:26.920
So it won't surprise any
of you that there are

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:30.780
many misconceptions about
what is and is not illegal.

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:33.120
The law is complicated and
it's easy to get confused

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:34.770
if you have no legal experience.

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:37.670
But the fun thing about law
is that people tend to believe

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:40.290
that there are certain hard
and fast rules that are

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:42.890
unfortunately, definitely wrong.

00:00:42.890 --> 00:00:46.240
So let's dispel some myths and
correct some misconceptions.

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:49.623
And be honest, did you
believe any of these things?

00:00:49.623 --> 00:00:50.770
(whooshing) Like, everybody knows

00:00:50.770 --> 00:00:52.783
it's illegal to marry your cousin.

00:00:52.783 --> 00:00:54.070
(buzzer buzzing) False.

00:00:54.070 --> 00:00:57.460
You can marry your cousin
provided it's your first cousin

00:00:57.460 --> 00:01:00.750
and you live in one of the 21
American states that allow it.

00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:03.943
Cousin marriage has always
been popular around the world.

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:06.480
Gross, but for hundreds of years,

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:08.480
it was considered a relatively good bet

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:10.070
for a long-lasting union.

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:13.290
Think of all the family
that you already share.

00:01:13.290 --> 00:01:15.890
But things started to change
in America in the mid 1800s

00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:18.440
when states decided that
cousins marrying cousins

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:20.890
was a bad idea, (whooshing)
and interestingly enough,

00:01:20.890 --> 00:01:23.620
cousin marriages weren't
ever outlawed in England

00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:25.830
and they are still allowed in Canada,

00:01:25.830 --> 00:01:27.130
largely because the practice was

00:01:27.130 --> 00:01:28.950
associated with the aristocracy.

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:31.400
In America, the situation
was largely the opposite.

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:33.610
Cousin marriage was
viewed negatively in part

00:01:33.610 --> 00:01:34.750
because it was practiced by people

00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:35.940
who didn't have lots of money,

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:38.310
but let's get down to brass tacks.

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:40.970
First cousin marriage is legal in Alabama,

00:01:40.970 --> 00:01:43.290
Alaska, California, Colorado, Connecticut,

00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:45.810
the District of Columbia,
Florida, because of course,

00:01:45.810 --> 00:01:48.440
Georgia, Hawaii, Maryland, Massachusetts,

00:01:48.440 --> 00:01:51.080
New Jersey, New Mexico, New
York, and North Carolina.

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:53.950
In several other states it's
allowed with some restrictions.

00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:56.760
These states include Arizona,
Illinois, Indiana, Utah,

00:01:56.760 --> 00:01:59.430
and Wisconsin, where first
cousin marriage is allowed

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:01.340
if the parties are above a certain age

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:04.150
or one is unable to produce.

00:02:04.150 --> 00:02:06.160
Maine also permits it if a couple obtains

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:08.600
a physician's certificate
of genetic counseling.

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:10.300
Though, note, if you
do go through with it,

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:13.040
Susan Collins may express her displeasure

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:16.156
and furrow an eyebrow, but
then allow you to do it anyway.

00:02:16.156 --> 00:02:18.860
- This is disgraceful!

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:20.440
- But I guess this so-called myth

00:02:20.440 --> 00:02:22.620
is actually true in certain states.

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:25.160
If you live in Arkansas,
Delaware, Idaho, Iowa,

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:27.610
Kansas, Kentucky, hey,
Kentucky, way to go,

00:02:27.610 --> 00:02:30.310
Louisiana, Michigan, Minnesota,
Mississippi, Missouri,

00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:33.030
Montana, Nebraska, Nevada,
New Hampshire, North Dakota,

00:02:33.030 --> 00:02:35.670
Ohio, Oklahoma, Oregon,
Pennsylvania, South Dakota,

00:02:35.670 --> 00:02:37.920
Washington, West Virginia, or Wyoming,

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:40.520
you'll have to look outside
of the family reunion

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:43.940
to find a spouse, which
brings us to the next myth.

00:02:43.940 --> 00:02:45.740
The reading of the will, (whooshing)

00:02:45.740 --> 00:02:47.810
because everyone knows when someone dies,

00:02:47.810 --> 00:02:49.530
you gather in a big ornate room,

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:52.130
and then you wait silently
until the executor

00:02:52.130 --> 00:02:54.150
reads your portion of
the will and hopefully,

00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:56.070
explaining that your estranged uncle

00:02:56.070 --> 00:02:57.360
left you millions of dollars,

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:00.180
or if you were expecting
millions of dollars, surprise,

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:03.850
you get nothing or you're left
with just a haunted mansion.

00:03:03.850 --> 00:03:06.330
This is (buzzer buzzing) false.

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:08.930
I'm willing to bet that no
one who is watching this video

00:03:08.930 --> 00:03:11.050
has ever been summoned
to a lawyer's office

00:03:11.050 --> 00:03:12.980
for the reading of a loved one's will.

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:14.940
Nevertheless, many people still believe

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:16.320
that when someone passes away,

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:19.040
there will be a family gathering
where a lawyer officially

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:21.860
breaks the news about who
will inherit money and assets.

00:03:21.860 --> 00:03:24.120
This is one of the most
reliable plot lines

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:27.180
for a daytime and nighttime soap opera.

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:30.943
- I'm left with 40%, they
are the major shareholders.

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:34.040
How did Daddy do this to me?

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:36.600
- The big event always takes
place in the lawyer's office

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:40.480
or old-timey library and
involves the whole crazy family,

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:41.760
all in one room together.

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:44.730
It's led by a well-dressed
lawyer wearing Indochino,

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:46.840
presumably, who gets to do the big reveal.

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:49.180
You can't blame screenwriters for wanting

00:03:49.180 --> 00:03:51.090
a reading of the will to be a real thing.

00:03:51.090 --> 00:03:53.480
It's a great way to
create dramatic tension.

00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:57.490
And there's a lot riding on
this dead person's estate.

00:03:57.490 --> 00:03:59.770
- [Woman] I'm leaving you my keyboard.

00:03:59.770 --> 00:04:02.430
I'm going to keep it in
my room at the Q Mansion.

00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:03.690
Please give it to Leo.

00:04:03.690 --> 00:04:05.080
- Will someone walk away rich?

00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:06.610
Will someone be disinherited?

00:04:06.610 --> 00:04:08.590
Will someone unexpected show up?

00:04:08.590 --> 00:04:10.000
Will there be a fight?

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:11.270
But here's the thing.

00:04:11.270 --> 00:04:14.240
There is no legal requirement
that a Last Will and Testament

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:15.820
be read aloud to family members,

00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:17.220
or anyone else for that matter.

00:04:17.220 --> 00:04:19.240
All beneficiaries named in a will are

00:04:19.240 --> 00:04:21.660
entitled to receive a copy
so they can understand

00:04:21.660 --> 00:04:23.330
what they'll be receiving from the estate,

00:04:23.330 --> 00:04:25.260
and when those assets will be distributed,

00:04:25.260 --> 00:04:27.340
but there's no requirement
that the executor

00:04:27.340 --> 00:04:29.580
usher everyone into a
room where the contents

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:31.130
of the will will be revealed.

00:04:31.130 --> 00:04:33.660
In fact, lawyers would
prefer not to do it this way

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:35.710
since we aren't trained to break up fights

00:04:35.710 --> 00:04:37.610
like a bouncer at Scores.

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:40.430
Lawyers prefer to fight with
our words, not our fists,

00:04:40.430 --> 00:04:43.355
and wound you with a pen
rather than the sword.

00:04:43.355 --> 00:04:45.000
(bell dinging)

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:47.640
So if there's no requirement
for wills to be read out loud,

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:49.420
where did this idea come from?

00:04:49.420 --> 00:04:50.940
Well, a hundred years ago,

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:53.210
estate attorneys did
gather the family together

00:04:53.210 --> 00:04:54.990
to read the will because not everyone

00:04:54.990 --> 00:04:57.010
could actually read the will on their own.

00:04:57.010 --> 00:05:00.090
Nowadays, we sort of assume
that the beneficiaries

00:05:00.090 --> 00:05:01.990
of a will are actually literate.

00:05:01.990 --> 00:05:05.090
But the main conflict in movies
and TV shows involving wills

00:05:05.090 --> 00:05:07.230
is whether someone will be disinherited.

00:05:07.230 --> 00:05:09.250
And this is definitely
a source of conflict

00:05:09.250 --> 00:05:10.083
in the real world.

00:05:10.083 --> 00:05:11.320
And since most states usually have

00:05:11.320 --> 00:05:14.110
a very strict timeframe
for contesting a will,

00:05:14.110 --> 00:05:16.110
it could be to everyone's
advantage to start the clock

00:05:16.110 --> 00:05:19.070
by notifying disinherited heirs
earlier, rather than later.

00:05:19.070 --> 00:05:20.090
This is actually what happened

00:05:20.090 --> 00:05:21.660
when President Trump's father died

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:24.130
because it's 2020 and everything involves

00:05:24.130 --> 00:05:25.190
Donald Trump these days.

00:05:25.190 --> 00:05:26.600
Fred Trump, (whooshing)
Donald Trump's father,

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:27.582
amended his will to disinherit

00:05:27.582 --> 00:05:29.290
Mary Trump and Fred Trump III,

00:05:29.290 --> 00:05:33.820
the descendants of Fred Trump
Jr. who died when he was 46.

00:05:33.820 --> 00:05:36.360
Mary and Fred were cut out of a 20% share

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:39.100
of their grandfather's estate
that they might have received

00:05:39.100 --> 00:05:40.270
had their father lived.

00:05:40.270 --> 00:05:43.010
The other Fred Trump grandchildren
are in line to inherit

00:05:43.010 --> 00:05:45.510
their parents' full shares
in Fred Trump's estate.

00:05:45.510 --> 00:05:47.560
Now the Trump children, including Donald,

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:49.670
told tax authorities that Dad's properties

00:05:49.670 --> 00:05:52.590
were worth around $41 million
at the time of his death.

00:05:52.590 --> 00:05:54.450
The "New York Times"
reported that the true value

00:05:54.450 --> 00:05:56.090
was closer to $1 billion,

00:05:56.090 --> 00:05:59.440
which obviously is a lot of
money to be disinherited from.

00:05:59.440 --> 00:06:02.830
Fred III and Mary filed a will
contest nine months later,

00:06:02.830 --> 00:06:05.410
which brings us to the
next legal misconception

00:06:05.410 --> 00:06:06.410
because everyone knows,

00:06:06.410 --> 00:06:09.190
if you go into a casino and
you start counting cards,

00:06:09.190 --> 00:06:10.280
you can be arrested (whooshing)

00:06:10.280 --> 00:06:12.652
because counting cards is illegal.

00:06:12.652 --> 00:06:14.180
(buzzer buzzing) False.

00:06:14.180 --> 00:06:15.950
Counting cards while playing blackjack,

00:06:15.950 --> 00:06:19.190
though widely considered
to be illegal, is not.

00:06:19.190 --> 00:06:21.040
Really, you're just playing the game

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:22.030
that the casino is offering

00:06:22.030 --> 00:06:24.110
at a much higher level
than they were expecting.

00:06:24.110 --> 00:06:26.460
There is no local state or federal law

00:06:26.460 --> 00:06:28.570
that makes counting cards against the law.

00:06:28.570 --> 00:06:30.410
Generally, the way it
works in blackjack is

00:06:30.410 --> 00:06:31.990
that it's more favorable for the player

00:06:31.990 --> 00:06:34.350
when there are more aces and cards

00:06:34.350 --> 00:06:36.770
that are valued at 10, the 10s, jacks,

00:06:36.770 --> 00:06:39.410
queens, and kings, remaining in the shoe.

00:06:39.410 --> 00:06:41.450
So card counting is simply using a system

00:06:41.450 --> 00:06:44.200
to keep track of the ratio
of low cards to high cards.

00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:45.730
And if you have a halfway decent memory,

00:06:45.730 --> 00:06:47.010
you can teach yourself to do this

00:06:47.010 --> 00:06:48.560
by tracking the changing imbalance

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:51.030
of big and small cards
in a diminishing deck.

00:06:51.030 --> 00:06:52.660
And since you're just using your brain,

00:06:52.660 --> 00:06:54.220
this is perfectly fine.

00:06:54.220 --> 00:06:56.070
There's even a court case making it legal,

00:06:56.070 --> 00:06:57.200
at least in New Jersey,

00:06:57.200 --> 00:07:00.270
because everything is legal in New Jersey.

00:07:00.270 --> 00:07:01.410
The New Jersey Supreme Court (whooshing)

00:07:01.410 --> 00:07:03.600
has ruled that players can
not be discriminated against

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:04.870
based on their playing skills,

00:07:04.870 --> 00:07:07.070
even if they are caught counting cards.

00:07:07.070 --> 00:07:08.710
Counting cards does not violate

00:07:08.710 --> 00:07:10.580
the New Jersey Casino Control Act.

00:07:10.580 --> 00:07:12.560
However, counting cards
becomes problematic

00:07:12.560 --> 00:07:14.260
if you're using a device to count cards,

00:07:14.260 --> 00:07:16.380
or you have friends who
are tipping you off.

00:07:16.380 --> 00:07:17.730
Though, if you're playing in Nevada,

00:07:17.730 --> 00:07:19.230
the rules are a bit different.

00:07:19.230 --> 00:07:21.500
There is no Nevada law
against counting cards,

00:07:21.500 --> 00:07:23.270
but the casinos also have free reign

00:07:23.270 --> 00:07:25.300
to remove people as they see fit.

00:07:25.300 --> 00:07:26.820
Casinos are private property.

00:07:26.820 --> 00:07:29.140
They can remove just about
anyone for any reason.

00:07:29.140 --> 00:07:31.570
So if you're caught
counting cards in Vegas,

00:07:31.570 --> 00:07:33.030
even with your brain,

00:07:33.030 --> 00:07:35.100
you can expect the pit
boss to pull you aside

00:07:35.100 --> 00:07:37.210
and prohibit you from playing blackjack,

00:07:37.210 --> 00:07:39.541
which takes us to the
next legal misconception

00:07:39.541 --> 00:07:41.600
(whooshing)because everybody
knows that you have to wait

00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:44.380
24 hours to report a missing person,

00:07:44.380 --> 00:07:46.680
even if you know that
your loved one is missing.

00:07:46.680 --> 00:07:48.720
You were there when the dark van

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:50.400
pulled up beside your loved one,

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:52.330
the sliding door opened and some henchmen

00:07:52.330 --> 00:07:54.780
who were wearing black shirts
that said "Henchmen" on it,

00:07:54.780 --> 00:07:56.494
grabbed your loved one,
and threw 'em the van,

00:07:56.494 --> 00:07:57.600
(motor running) and drove off.

00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:00.020
And then of course, you go
to the local chief of police,

00:08:00.020 --> 00:08:01.720
who, of course, you
trust because he's been

00:08:01.720 --> 00:08:03.300
a long-time family friend,

00:08:03.300 --> 00:08:05.360
and you confide and say that you saw them

00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:06.890
abduct your loved one.

00:08:06.890 --> 00:08:09.657
And he says, "Look, I'm
sorry, I believe you,

00:08:09.657 --> 00:08:11.107
"but I can't do anything about it

00:08:11.107 --> 00:08:13.087
"because you have to wait 24 hours

00:08:13.087 --> 00:08:15.200
"before you can report a missing person."

00:08:15.200 --> 00:08:16.320
And it sounds reasonable.

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:17.820
It sounds like a law that actually exists,

00:08:17.820 --> 00:08:19.270
but of course, your
long-time family friend

00:08:19.270 --> 00:08:21.460
has been in on the conspiracy
from the very beginning.

00:08:21.460 --> 00:08:22.323
So obviously then you have to take the law

00:08:22.323 --> 00:08:24.470
into your own hands
because you waited 24 hours

00:08:24.470 --> 00:08:25.303
to report a missing person,

00:08:25.303 --> 00:08:28.442
but that (buzzer buzzing) is false.

00:08:28.442 --> 00:08:30.692
(laughing)

00:08:31.660 --> 00:08:32.530
(popping)

00:08:32.530 --> 00:08:34.660
A lot of people think that
if a person is missing,

00:08:34.660 --> 00:08:37.100
you have to wait 24 hours to
report it to the authorities.

00:08:37.100 --> 00:08:39.540
But in reality, there is
no state or federal law

00:08:39.540 --> 00:08:41.560
requiring police
departments to wait 24 hours

00:08:41.560 --> 00:08:43.640
before investigating a
missing persons case.

00:08:43.640 --> 00:08:46.360
In fact, in 1990, the Crime
Control Act became law,

00:08:46.360 --> 00:08:49.130
which mandated that all law
enforcement agencies immediately

00:08:49.130 --> 00:08:51.540
report a missing child under the age of 18

00:08:51.540 --> 00:08:53.170
to the National Crime Information Center

00:08:53.170 --> 00:08:54.300
at the Department of Justice.

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:56.560
And in 2003, President George W. Bush

00:08:56.560 --> 00:08:57.670
signed the Protect Act,

00:08:57.670 --> 00:09:00.970
which included Suzanne's
Law raising the age to 21.

00:09:00.970 --> 00:09:03.700
So anyone under that age could be reported

00:09:03.700 --> 00:09:05.130
to the National Database immediately.

00:09:05.130 --> 00:09:07.810
Suzanne Lyall was a
19-year-old college student

00:09:07.810 --> 00:09:09.270
who disappeared in 1998.

00:09:09.270 --> 00:09:10.930
And when Lyall's disappearance

00:09:10.930 --> 00:09:12.520
was reported to campus police,

00:09:12.520 --> 00:09:15.020
officers told her parents
that brief absences

00:09:15.020 --> 00:09:17.310
were not uncommon for college students.

00:09:17.310 --> 00:09:20.040
So they shouldn't worry since
she would probably turn up.

00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:21.870
She was unfortunately never seen again.

00:09:21.870 --> 00:09:23.480
And as Suzanne's case demonstrates,

00:09:23.480 --> 00:09:25.850
there is some truth to the
myth that for missing adults,

00:09:25.850 --> 00:09:27.670
police departments once
had waiting periods

00:09:27.670 --> 00:09:30.080
before they would take a
missing persons report.

00:09:30.080 --> 00:09:32.070
The common belief was that most people

00:09:32.070 --> 00:09:34.790
who seemed to be missing
actually returned fairly quickly.

00:09:34.790 --> 00:09:36.470
Today most law enforcement agencies

00:09:36.470 --> 00:09:38.860
have reversed course, even for adults.

00:09:38.860 --> 00:09:41.480
This is partly because in
the age of the cell phone,

00:09:41.480 --> 00:09:43.650
most people are reachable
relatively quickly.

00:09:43.650 --> 00:09:46.960
And if they don't respond,
something is likely to be wrong.

00:09:46.960 --> 00:09:49.640
But to sum it up, when an
adult or child goes missing,

00:09:49.640 --> 00:09:51.650
it should be immediately reported.

00:09:51.650 --> 00:09:52.890
And under Suzanne's Law,

00:09:52.890 --> 00:09:54.500
federal, state and local law enforcement

00:09:54.500 --> 00:09:57.030
must report all missing
persons up to the age of 21

00:09:57.030 --> 00:09:58.590
to the DOJ without delay,

00:09:58.590 --> 00:10:01.050
and the police are obligated
to investigate right away,

00:10:01.050 --> 00:10:03.770
which takes us to the
next legal misconception,

00:10:03.770 --> 00:10:06.042
which is that if you
are accused of a crime,

00:10:06.042 --> 00:10:08.520
(whooshing) everyone knows
that you can't be convicted

00:10:08.520 --> 00:10:10.620
on circumstantial evidence alone

00:10:10.620 --> 00:10:12.810
because everyone knows
circumstantial evidence is weak

00:10:12.810 --> 00:10:14.369
and can't lead to a conviction.

00:10:14.369 --> 00:10:16.070
(buzzer buzzing) False.

00:10:16.070 --> 00:10:18.197
Criminal defendants
often tell their lawyers,

00:10:18.197 --> 00:10:20.977
"All the prosecutors have on
me is circumstantial evidence.

00:10:20.977 --> 00:10:23.470
"And you can't convict on
circumstantial evidence alone."

00:10:23.470 --> 00:10:25.200
But the number one mistake people make

00:10:25.200 --> 00:10:27.940
about criminal law and frankly,
civil law for that matter,

00:10:27.940 --> 00:10:30.840
is that circumstantial evidence
is not enough to convict.

00:10:30.840 --> 00:10:32.930
But there's no law saying that.

00:10:32.930 --> 00:10:35.630
And in fact, the law says
the absolute opposite.

00:10:35.630 --> 00:10:37.470
The standard of proof
(whooshing) in criminal trials is

00:10:37.470 --> 00:10:38.690
beyond a reasonable doubt.

00:10:38.690 --> 00:10:40.410
And that means that the
prosecution must prove

00:10:40.410 --> 00:10:42.420
that the defendant is
guilty of a crime charged

00:10:42.420 --> 00:10:44.800
to the extent that no
reasonable person could have

00:10:44.800 --> 00:10:47.220
a reasonable doubt that
the defendant is guilty.

00:10:47.220 --> 00:10:49.070
And the prosecution can prove guilt

00:10:49.070 --> 00:10:50.850
beyond a reasonable
doubt by building a case

00:10:50.850 --> 00:10:52.860
through direct evidence,
circumstantial evidence,

00:10:52.860 --> 00:10:54.720
or a combination of both.

00:10:54.720 --> 00:10:57.740
Direct evidence directly
shows the defendant's guilt

00:10:57.740 --> 00:10:59.670
requiring no inference whatsoever.

00:10:59.670 --> 00:11:02.720
Direct evidence would be
a video showing someone is

00:11:02.720 --> 00:11:04.860
killing the victim or a witness testifying

00:11:04.860 --> 00:11:07.060
that they saw the person
murdering that victim.

00:11:07.060 --> 00:11:08.620
Circumstantial evidence on the other hand

00:11:08.620 --> 00:11:11.170
is indirect evidence that
may lead to the inference

00:11:11.170 --> 00:11:12.560
that the defendant committed a crime,

00:11:12.560 --> 00:11:14.600
even though there's no direct proof.

00:11:14.600 --> 00:11:16.500
That key word here is inference,

00:11:16.500 --> 00:11:19.700
since circumstantial evidence
allows jurors to infer facts

00:11:19.700 --> 00:11:22.500
that the prosecutors don't
have direct evidence to prove.

00:11:22.500 --> 00:11:25.410
But circumstantial evidence
can be extremely strong.

00:11:25.410 --> 00:11:26.920
Even DNA evidence,

00:11:26.920 --> 00:11:28.910
considered some of the
strongest evidence out there,

00:11:28.910 --> 00:11:30.150
is circumstantial.

00:11:30.150 --> 00:11:32.800
For example, if a criminal
defendant had traces

00:11:32.800 --> 00:11:35.690
of the victim's blood in their apartment,

00:11:35.690 --> 00:11:37.340
then you might make the inference

00:11:37.340 --> 00:11:39.760
that that person was in fact, the killer,

00:11:39.760 --> 00:11:41.420
though it's possible that that blood

00:11:41.420 --> 00:11:43.560
could have gotten there
under other circumstances.

00:11:43.560 --> 00:11:46.270
So that might be
extremely strong evidence,

00:11:46.270 --> 00:11:48.130
but there might be an explanation for it.

00:11:48.130 --> 00:11:49.750
That's circumstantial evidence.

00:11:49.750 --> 00:11:52.310
Of course, not all circumstantial
evidence is created equal.

00:11:52.310 --> 00:11:54.390
Some is stronger and weaker than others.

00:11:54.390 --> 00:11:56.600
And this is where jury
discretion comes in.

00:11:56.600 --> 00:11:58.410
It's up to the individual jurors to weigh

00:11:58.410 --> 00:12:00.520
the importance of each piece of evidence,

00:12:00.520 --> 00:12:03.020
and then decide together
whether the prosecution

00:12:03.020 --> 00:12:04.080
has proven his case.

00:12:04.080 --> 00:12:05.820
For example, there was no direct evidence

00:12:05.820 --> 00:12:08.340
that infamous Casey Anthony
killed her daughter Caylee,

00:12:08.340 --> 00:12:09.980
but the prosecution thought they had

00:12:09.980 --> 00:12:11.270
strong circumstantial evidence.

00:12:11.270 --> 00:12:13.480
Their theory was that
Casey used chloroform

00:12:13.480 --> 00:12:14.940
to suffocate her child.

00:12:14.940 --> 00:12:17.130
However, the child's body
was so badly decomposed

00:12:17.130 --> 00:12:19.100
that there was no definitive determination

00:12:19.100 --> 00:12:19.933
of the cause of death.

00:12:19.933 --> 00:12:22.160
The prosecution had evidence that someone

00:12:22.160 --> 00:12:24.800
in Anthony's household
had done internet searches

00:12:24.800 --> 00:12:26.300
for information on chloroform,

00:12:26.300 --> 00:12:28.340
and traces of chloroform were found

00:12:28.340 --> 00:12:29.750
in the trunk of Anthony's car.

00:12:29.750 --> 00:12:31.810
And the prosecution hoped
that the jury would draw

00:12:31.810 --> 00:12:34.280
an inference that the internet
search in chloroform traces,

00:12:34.280 --> 00:12:36.280
along with the fact that
Anthony didn't report

00:12:36.280 --> 00:12:37.990
her child was missing for a month,

00:12:37.990 --> 00:12:39.440
prove that she killed her child.

00:12:39.440 --> 00:12:41.830
And of course, a lot of
people believe that as well.

00:12:41.830 --> 00:12:44.420
However, the jury did not
think that that was enough

00:12:44.420 --> 00:12:46.990
to prove that Casey
Anthony killed her daughter

00:12:46.990 --> 00:12:48.100
beyond a reasonable doubt.

00:12:48.100 --> 00:12:50.620
If the same case was tried
to 10 different juries,

00:12:50.620 --> 00:12:52.160
it'd be interesting to
know how many of them

00:12:52.160 --> 00:12:53.830
would have drawn the same conclusion,

00:12:53.830 --> 00:12:55.670
but that's not how our system works.

00:12:55.670 --> 00:12:57.960
You can be convicted or found not guilty

00:12:57.960 --> 00:13:00.270
based on circumstantial evidence alone,

00:13:00.270 --> 00:13:02.810
but that brings us to the
next legal misconception,

00:13:02.810 --> 00:13:05.050
which is that everybody
knows that if you're doing

00:13:05.050 --> 00:13:06.980
something illegal and
you think you might be

00:13:06.980 --> 00:13:08.750
interacting with an undercover cop,

00:13:08.750 --> 00:13:11.225
you should ask them if
they are a police officer,

00:13:11.225 --> 00:13:14.492
(whooshing) because everyone
knows police can't lie to you.

00:13:14.492 --> 00:13:15.515
(buzzer buzzing) False.

00:13:15.515 --> 00:13:18.450
Our final misconception
is that police cannot lie

00:13:18.450 --> 00:13:20.110
to you during an interrogation.

00:13:20.110 --> 00:13:22.900
Actually, they absolutely
can use deception.

00:13:22.900 --> 00:13:24.615
There's actually a
Supreme Court case on it.

00:13:24.615 --> 00:13:27.130
(whooshing) In Frazier vs Cupp,
the Supreme Court affirmed

00:13:27.130 --> 00:13:29.930
the legality of deceptive
interrogation tactics.

00:13:29.930 --> 00:13:32.120
Police in that case got a
tip that Martin Frazier,

00:13:32.120 --> 00:13:34.600
a 20-year-old U.S.
Marine, might be involved

00:13:34.600 --> 00:13:36.820
in the murder of Russell Anton Marleau.

00:13:36.820 --> 00:13:39.890
Frazier, along with his
cousin, Jerry Lee Rawls,

00:13:39.890 --> 00:13:42.250
were seen at a bar with the
victim before the murder.

00:13:42.250 --> 00:13:43.480
During the interrogation,

00:13:43.480 --> 00:13:46.030
the police falsely informed
Frazier that Rawls had

00:13:46.030 --> 00:13:48.870
already confessed and
implicated him in the murder.

00:13:48.870 --> 00:13:50.940
Frazier denied any
involvement in the crime

00:13:50.940 --> 00:13:52.700
and suggested speaking with an attorney.

00:13:52.700 --> 00:13:54.610
But police continued to question him.

00:13:54.610 --> 00:13:56.420
Police got Frazier to confess.

00:13:56.420 --> 00:13:58.950
This confession was used
against him at trial,

00:13:58.950 --> 00:14:00.770
and he was convicted of murder.

00:14:00.770 --> 00:14:02.960
Rawls also pleaded guilty
to the same offense.

00:14:02.960 --> 00:14:04.850
The Supreme Court
rejected Frazier's appeal

00:14:04.850 --> 00:14:06.810
stating that although
police misrepresented

00:14:06.810 --> 00:14:07.710
Rawls's testimony,

00:14:07.710 --> 00:14:09.860
that did not make the
confession inadmissible.

00:14:09.860 --> 00:14:11.580
And later, case law has interpreted this

00:14:11.580 --> 00:14:14.020
as allowing deception
during interrogations.

00:14:14.020 --> 00:14:16.750
This includes lying about
evidence like fingerprints or DNA.

00:14:16.750 --> 00:14:18.430
However, there are some limits.

00:14:18.430 --> 00:14:19.340
Police, for example,

00:14:19.340 --> 00:14:21.930
can't misrepresent a
person's basic legal rights.

00:14:21.930 --> 00:14:23.560
So if you don't know, now you know.

